## 10. Design Decisions (DRAFTED — flagged for the architect to RATIFY)

### [DD-CIC-STATION-IDENTITY] The duty-station identity + peripheral-pairing scheme

- decision: how a duty station is identified and how a peripheral binds to it.
- chosen: a station = a `{name, uuid}` whose SCOPE TOKEN is a dotted SEMANTIC path of the form `location.instance.role` (e.g. `raptor.1.pilot`, `sickbay.patient1`, `cic.1.helm`) — the SHAPE is fixed (a dotted hierarchical path, location-first), the values are open (not an enum); every colonial_console namespace carries `colonial_console/<station>/...` under the GENERALIZED `colonial_console/` scope root (generalized from the CIC-specific `cic/` root now that the widget is the fleet-wide `colonial_console`); a peripheral keeps its own `_device/<id>` identity ([CXC-ER-RESTART-BUS]) and is PAIRED to a station by a `station: <station>` config binding, after which it publishes to that station's namespaces and announces at `colonial_console/<station>/peripherals/<id>`. The station is a higher-level grouping (display + physical controls) than a single device. The station's `location` segment and its `theme` ([CXC-CIC-WIDGET]) often correlate but are DISTINCT bindings.
- rationale: station-scoping every namespace is the ONLY thing that keeps multiple stations + their peripherals off each other's wires on one bus ([CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION]); reusing `_device/<id>` for device identity and adding a `station:` binding for the grouping avoids inventing a second device-identity scheme. Prefix pattern-subscription (`colonial_console/<station>/*`) makes the isolation a bus-native filter ([SPEC:CXC-PATSUB]), not a client-side discard.
- alternatives: (a) a flat global `colonial_console/console` with a station field in the payload — rejected: every station receives every selection and filters in the leaf, defeating [CXC-PS-INTENT]; (b) fold the station into `_device/<id>` — rejected: a station is a group of devices (a display + two rotaries), not one device.
- invalidating_assumption: that gameplay wants multiple independent stations on one bus. If the program stays single-station, the `<station>` scope collapses to the `station-0` default and pairing is trivial — but the scope is cheap insurance that a multi-station build does not require a namespace migration.
- status: proposed
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CIC]

### [DD-CIC-SVG-KIND] The two artwork-kinds (primitive vs raster) + the tag/infer contract

- decision: how the scan decides whether an SVG element becomes a native primitive or a baked raster asset.
- chosen: every element carries an ARTWORK-KIND — RENDER-AS-PRIMITIVE (vector geometry -> engine Canvas paths / bound widget, crisp + animatable + bindable) or EXTRACT-AS-RASTER (baked to CI16/PNG). David may TAG each element explicitly; else the scan INFERS (`*-image`/photo -> raster; vector strokes/fills -> primitive); an explicit tag wins. The reticule (+bullseye/indicators) is a PRIMITIVE with labeled parts, never bitmapped.
- rationale: the reticule must animate + bind to the focused-planet namespace (the DRADIS rotation-binding precedent) — a bitmap cannot do that, so it MUST stay a primitive; planet photos have no vector form, so they MUST be raster. A per-element kind (tag-or-infer) is the deterministic contract that lets David author screen-by-screen without the scan guessing wrong. This directly resolves David's "combine the reticule for extraction?" — no: it is never extracted.
- alternatives: (a) extract everything to raster (rejected: kills animation/binding/crispness on the reticule + bars); (b) render everything as primitives (rejected: planet photos + painterly textures have no vector form).
- invalidating_assumption: that the reticule + resource bars remain vector-expressible and the planet art remains photographic. If a screen needs an animated raster or a vector planet, the tag overrides the inference — the contract already supports the per-element override.
- status: proposed
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CIC]

### [DD-CIC-TWO-UP] The 2-up portrait multi-instance mechanism

- decision: how two independent `colonial_console` instances coexist on one portrait-mounted display.
- chosen: ONE runtime scene spanning the portrait display, stacking two `colonial_console` instances in a vertical `pixel_stack`/column, each bound to a DISTINCT station id, each applying its own frame mask within its region; the plywood two-square physical mask crops each 4:3 design into its square. Per-region fit defaults to letterbox-to-fit.
- rationale: station-scoping ([CXC-CIC-DUTY-STATION]) already isolates the two instances' namespaces, so two instances in one scene is the minimal move — one process, one display, two stations — and the frame-as-master-mask handles each region's bounds. Two separate windows/processes would duplicate display management for no isolation gain (the bus scope already isolates them).
- alternatives: (a) two separate runtime windows/processes tiled on the display (more process management, same isolation the bus scope already gives); (b) one instance with a "split" mode (rejected: forks the shell to serve a layout the composition model already expresses as two instances).
- invalidating_assumption: that the two squares are independent stations (David's brief). If the two squares are ever ONE station's two views, this collapses to a single instance with two regions — but the brief says independent duty stations. The letterbox-vs-crop fit into the plywood square is the open sub-call flagged for ratify.
- status: proposed
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CIC]

### [DD-CIC-TIER] The embedded-tier stance for the CIC shell

- decision: whether the full `colonial_console` is an embedded-tier capability.
- chosen: the FULL 7x5 shell is TOUCHSCREEN/DESKTOP-FIRST — a WAIVER from embedded parity with a recorded reason ([CXC-CIC-TIER]); a SINGLE reduced console screen (e.g. DRADIS alone) is a legitimate embedded target as a distinct reduced variant, NOT a claimed port of the full shell.
- rationale: the shell's value (two-level nav + ambient animation + mask composite on a full touch display) is a console/desktop surface; a constrained device has neither the budget nor the touch real estate for a 7x5 two-tab-row console. But a single content scene already runs embedded ([CXC-ER-DRADIS-BUS] / [CXC-ER-RENDER]), so a reduced single-screen variant is real — it is just a different, smaller capability. Recording the stance (not omitting it) satisfies the tier-parity escape hatch ([CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY]).
- alternatives: (a) claim full embedded parity (rejected: a 7x5 animated CIC exceeds the ESP32 budget — an over-claim); (b) console-only with NO embedded path (rejected: DRADIS already proves a single reduced screen renders embedded, so a blanket exclusion under-claims).
- invalidating_assumption: that the constrained tier cannot host the full two-level nav + ambient composite. If a Qualia-S3-class full-framebuffer surface makes the full shell tractable embedded, the waiver is revisited and the full shell targets that tier.
- status: proposed
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CIC]

### [DD-CIC-THEME] The colonial-console UX is fleet-wide + re-themable — a SEMANTIC `theme` binding, and the widget renamed to `colonial_console` (RATIFIED)

- decision: whether the `standard_ux_shape_element` colonial-console UX is CIC-specific or a fleet-wide, re-themable shell; HOW theming is bound (raw color vs a semantic name); and what to do about the CIC-specific widget name.
- chosen: the shell is a FLEET-WIDE THEMEABLE colonial-console; CIC is ONE instance (the DEFAULT theme). Theming is a SEMANTIC binding: a `theme` token ∈ {`cic` (default), `sickbay`, `flight_deck`, `raptor`, …extensible} is the PRIMARY interface, and the shell RESOLVES `theme` -> a (`base_color`, `highlight_color`) palette (`cic` -> `#762400` / `#CD671D`, the landed amber; sickbay/flight_deck/raptor -> TBD palettes). `base_color`/`highlight_color` remain BINDABLE as the RESOLVED output tokens and as a raw one-off override, but the semantic `theme` is the front door — named values, not raw hex ([CXC-CIC-WIDGET] theming). The widget is RENAMED to the generic `colonial_console` and the namespace scope root generalized from `cic/` to `colonial_console/`.
- naming-generalization (RATIFIED + ACTIONED 2026-07-30): because the UX is fleet-wide, the widget carries the GENERIC name `colonial_console` with CIC / sickbay / flight_deck / raptor as themed INSTANCES. This amendment ACTIONS that rename in the PROSE + params (`cic_widget` -> `colonial_console`) and generalizes the namespace scope root (`cic/<station>/*` -> `colonial_console/<station>/*`). The NODE IDs and the DOC TAG are DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED — [SPEC:CXC-CIC] and every [CXC-CIC-*] / [DD-CIC-*] id stay EXACTLY as landed, because they are stable cross-reference targets (the decision graph — [DEC-CONVERTED-CAROUSEL-IS-THE-BSG-SIM-SHELL], [DEC-CAROUSEL-PLATFORM-CALLS-SETTLED] — and sibling specs point at them; renaming them would dangle those refs). The doc is named for its flagship use (CIC) but specs the generic `colonial_console`; CIC is the default theme.
- rationale: the same colonial-console aesthetic recurs across many ship locations differing only in color, so the palette belongs on the SHELL, not baked into a CIC-specific constant — one shell, N themes, is strictly cheaper than N forked widgets and matches the reuse-by-reference discipline. A SEMANTIC `theme` name (not raw hex) is the right front door because a station operator/author reasons in named locations ("this is a raptor console"), not hex triples; the name resolves to a palette the shell owns, so palettes can be tuned centrally without touching every binding, while a raw `base_color`/`highlight_color` override stays available for a one-off. The generic `colonial_console` name matches the fleet-wide reality; keeping the node/doc ids stable keeps the rename a prose+param move, not a corpus-wide ref migration.
- alternatives: (a) keep the palette CIC-specific and fork a new widget per location (rejected: N forks of one shell for a color change, exactly the duplication reuse-by-reference forbids); (b) expose ONLY raw `base_color`/`highlight_color` with no semantic layer (rejected: forces every station to carry hex and scatters the palette across bindings — the semantic `theme` name centralizes it); (c) rename the node/doc ids too (rejected: they are stable cross-reference targets; renaming dangles the decision graph + sibling specs — the prose/param rename gets the generalization without the ref breakage).
- invalidating_assumption: that the fleet's colonial consoles vary ONLY by resolved palette over one shared structure. If a location needs a structurally different console (different frame, different nav), that is a different widget, not a theme — and the `theme` seam does not cover it. Also assumes a fixed set of named themes is workable; the token is extensible, so a new location adds a `theme` value + a palette entry, not a fork.
- resolution: RATIFIED by David 2026-07-30 — rename the widget (prose + params) to `colonial_console`, adopt the semantic `theme` binding, and generalize the namespace scope root to `colonial_console/`. Node ids + doc tag [SPEC:CXC-CIC] unchanged. OPEN sub-item flagged for David: the `sickbay` / `flight_deck` / `raptor` theme palettes are named-but-TBD (the `sickbay` green `#1B5E20` / `#66BB6A` is a placeholder from the worked scenario, not ratified).
- status: accepted
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-CIC]

